\title{A Spectral Gram-Matrix Bound for Uniform Set Families:\\
Fisher / Frankl--Wilson Inequalities via Heged\H{u}s' Eigenvalue Condition}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-28}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a self-contained, fully formalized treatment of a Fisher / Frankl--Wilson type
extremal bound for uniform set families, derived entirely from a single linear-algebraic
fact: the positive definiteness of a constant-pattern Gram matrix. Given a family of $m$
subsets of an $n$-element ground set in which each set has size $k$ and any two distinct
members meet in exactly $\lambda$ points with $\lambda < k$, we prove that $m \le n$. The
argument proceeds through an explicit \emph{combinatorics--algebra dictionary}: each set is
mapped to its $0/1$ incidence vector in $\R^n$, the inner product of two such vectors equals
the size of the intersection of the underlying sets, and the resulting Gram matrix is exactly
the constant-pattern matrix $(k-\lambda)I + \lambda J$. Heged\H{u}s' eigenvalue condition---positive
definiteness of this matrix---follows from an elementary additive decomposition into a strictly
positive-definite scalar multiple of the identity and a positive-semidefinite all-ones matrix,
requiring no diagonalization. Positive definiteness of the Gram matrix forces linear independence
of the incidence vectors, and a dimension count in $\R^n$ yields the bound. We exhibit the
singleton family as a verified tight instance ($k=1$, $\lambda=0$, $m=n$), establish the necessity
of the hypothesis $\lambda < k$ through the spectral degeneracy at $\lambda = k$, and state the
unindexed family form bridged to a general uniformity predicate. We close with four conjectural
extensions: non-uniform Fisher via diagonal-dominant Gram positivity, modular Heged\H{u}s bounds
over $\F_p$, equiangular-lines bounds from eigenvalue gaps, and quantitative refinements.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Fisher inequality, Frankl--Wilson, Heged\H{u}s eigenvalue condition,
Gram matrix, positive definiteness, incidence vector, uniform set family, spectral method,
equiangular lines, extremal combinatorics.

\section{Introduction}

A recurring theme in extremal combinatorics is that a strong \emph{uniformity} constraint on a
family of sets forces the family to be small. The prototype is \textbf{Fisher's inequality}: in a
balanced incomplete block design where any two distinct blocks meet in exactly $\lambda$ points, the
number of blocks is at least the number of points. The dual and its many relatives---the
Frankl--Wilson theorems, the de~Bruijn--Erd\H{o}s theorem, the nonuniform Ray-Chaudhuri--Wilson
bound, and Heged\H{u}s' eigenvalue formulations---share a single proof technique known as the
\textbf{linear-algebra method}: encode the combinatorial objects as vectors and bound their number
by the dimension of the ambient space.

This paper isolates the cleanest such bound and presents it as a corollary of one eigenvalue
inequality.

\begin{theorem}[Uniform Fisher bound]\label{thm:intro}
Let $A_1, \dots, A_m \subseteq \{1, \dots, n\}$ satisfy $|A_i| = k$ for all $i$ and
$|A_i \cap A_j| = \lambda$ for all $i \ne j$, where $\lambda < k$. Then $m \le n$.
\end{theorem}

The proof is a four-step pipeline:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Dictionary.} Map each $A_i$ to its incidence vector $v_i \in \R^n$; then
        $\inner{v_i}{v_j} = |A_i \cap A_j|$.
  \item \textbf{Gram identification.} The Gram matrix $G = (\inner{v_i}{v_j})_{i,j}$ equals
        $(k-\lambda)I + \lambda J$.
  \item \textbf{Spectral fact (Heged\H{u}s' condition).} For $0 \le \lambda < k$, the matrix
        $(k-\lambda)I + \lambda J$ is positive definite.
  \item \textbf{Dimension count.} A positive-definite Gram matrix certifies linear independence of
        $\{v_i\}$, and $\R^n$ holds at most $n$ independent vectors, so $m \le n$.
\end{enumerate}

The contribution of this work is a machine-checked, modular rendering of this pipeline in which the
spectral engine is fully decoupled from the combinatorial vocabulary, so that the same engine drives
both an indexed and an unindexed statement, and so that the necessity of $\lambda < k$ is exhibited as
the precise point of spectral degeneracy.

\section{Definitions and Notation}

Throughout, $n, m, k, \lambda$ are natural numbers, and the ground set is $[n] = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$,
identified with the finite type $\Fin n$. We work in the Euclidean space $\R^n$ with its standard
inner product $\inner{\cdot}{\cdot}$.

\begin{definition}[Incidence vector]
For a subset $A \subseteq [n]$, its \emph{incidence vector} $v_A \in \R^n$ is the $0/1$ indicator of
membership:
\[
v_A(t) = \begin{cases} 1 & t \in A, \\ 0 & t \notin A. \end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform family]
A family $\mathcal{F}$ of subsets of $[n]$ is \emph{$k$-uniform} if $|A| = k$ for every
$A \in \mathcal{F}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Constant-pattern matrix]
For real scalars $a, b$ and a size $m$, the \emph{constant-pattern matrix} is $aI + bJ$, where $I$
is the $m \times m$ identity and $J$ is the $m \times m$ all-ones matrix. Its diagonal entries are
$a + b$ and its off-diagonal entries are $b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Gram matrix]
For vectors $v_1, \dots, v_m \in \R^n$, the \emph{Gram matrix} is $G \in \R^{m \times m}$ with
$G_{ij} = \inner{v_i}{v_j}$. $G$ is always symmetric and positive semidefinite; it is positive
definite iff $v_1, \dots, v_m$ are linearly independent.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Positive definiteness]
A symmetric real matrix $M$ is \emph{positive definite} if $x^\top M x > 0$ for every nonzero
vector $x$, equivalently if all eigenvalues of $M$ are strictly positive. It is \emph{positive
semidefinite} if $x^\top M x \ge 0$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

\section{The Combinatorics--Algebra Dictionary}

The entire reduction hinges on a single identity translating set intersection into inner product.

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{incidence\_inner}]\label{lem:inner}
For all subsets $A, B \subseteq [n]$, $\inner{v_A}{v_B} = |A \cap B|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By the coordinate formula for the standard inner product,
\[
\inner{v_A}{v_B} = \sum_{t \in [n]} v_A(t)\, v_B(t) = \sum_{t \in [n]} [t \in A][t \in B],
\]
where $[\,\cdot\,]$ is the Iverson bracket. The product $[t\in A][t\in B]$ equals $1$ exactly when
$t \in A \cap B$ and $0$ otherwise, so the sum collapses to $\sum_t [t \in A \cap B] = |A \cap B|$.
Formally, this is a sum congruence rewriting each summand to $[t \in A \cap B]$, followed by
evaluating the indicator sum as the cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{incidence\_inner\_self}]\label{lem:innerself}
For all $A \subseteq [n]$, $\inner{v_A}{v_A} = |A|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Specialize Lemma~\ref{lem:inner} to $B = A$; then $A \cap A = A$, so
$\inner{v_A}{v_A} = |A \cap A| = |A|$.
\end{proof}

These two lemmas are the only place where combinatorics enters. Everything downstream is linear
algebra.

\section{The Spectral Engine}

\begin{theorem}[Constant-pattern positive definiteness, \texttt{constPattern\_posDef}]\label{thm:posdef}
Let $0 \le \lambda < k$ be reals and $m \ge 1$. Then the $m \times m$ matrix
$M = (k - \lambda)\, I + \lambda\, J$ is positive definite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Decompose $M$ additively as $M = (k-\lambda) I + \lambda J$. For any vector $x$,
\[
x^\top M x = (k - \lambda)\, \|x\|^2 + \lambda \Big(\sum_i x_i\Big)^2.
\]
Since $\lambda \ge 0$, the second term is a nonnegative multiple of a square, so $\lambda J$ is
positive semidefinite. Since $k - \lambda > 0$, the first term satisfies $(k-\lambda)\|x\|^2 > 0$
for every nonzero $x$, so $(k-\lambda)I$ is positive definite. The sum of a positive-definite and a
positive-semidefinite matrix is positive definite. No diagonalization is required; the eigenvalues
are $k + (m-1)\lambda$ (eigenvector all-ones) and $k - \lambda$ (multiplicity $m-1$), both positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral size bound, \texttt{gram\_posDef\_card\_le} / \texttt{constGram\_card\_le}]\label{thm:bound}
Let $v_1, \dots, v_m \in \R^n$ and suppose the Gram matrix $G_{ij} = \inner{v_i}{v_j}$ is positive
definite. Then $m \le n$. In the constant-Gram form: if $\inner{v_i}{v_i} = k$ for all $i$,
$\inner{v_i}{v_j} = \lambda$ for all $i \ne j$, with $0 \le \lambda < k$, then $m \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The Gram matrix equals $(k-\lambda)I + \lambda J$, which is positive definite by
Theorem~\ref{thm:posdef}. A positive-definite Gram matrix has trivial kernel: if
$\sum_i c_i v_i = 0$ then $c^\top G c = \big\langle \sum_i c_i v_i, \sum_j c_j v_j \big\rangle = 0$,
forcing $c = 0$. Hence $v_1, \dots, v_m$ are linearly independent in $\R^n$, and any independent set
has at most $\dim \R^n = n$ elements, giving $m \le n$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Main Fisher Bounds}

\begin{theorem}[Indexed uniform Fisher bound, \texttt{indexed\_fisher\_card\_le}]\label{thm:indexed}
Let $A : \Fin m \to \mathcal{P}([n])$ with $|A_i| = k$ for all $i$ and $|A_i \cap A_j| = \lambda$ for
all $i \ne j$, where $\lambda < k$. Then $m \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $v_i = v_{A_i}$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:innerself}, $\inner{v_i}{v_i} = |A_i| = k$; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:inner}, $\inner{v_i}{v_j} = |A_i \cap A_j| = \lambda$ for $i \ne j$. The casts give
$0 \le \lambda$ and $\lambda < k$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:bound}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finset-family Fisher bound, \texttt{isUniform\_fisher\_card\_le}]\label{thm:finset}
Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a finite family of subsets of $[n]$ that is $k$-uniform and satisfies
$|A \cap B| = \lambda$ for all distinct $A, B \in \mathcal{F}$, with $\lambda < k$. Then
$|\mathcal{F}| \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Enumerate $\mathcal{F}$ by a bijection $e : \Fin |\mathcal{F}| \xrightarrow{\sim} \mathcal{F}$ and set
$A_i = e(i)$. Uniformity gives $|A_i| = k$; injectivity of $e$ makes distinct indices yield distinct
sets, transporting the intersection hypothesis. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:indexed}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:finset} is the cross-domain bridge: it consumes a general uniformity predicate from
the combinatorics layer and discharges it through the spectral engine.

\section{Sharpness and Necessity}

\subsection{Sharpness: the singleton family}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{singletonFamily}]
The singleton family is $S : \Fin n \to \mathcal{P}([n])$, $S_i = \{i\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{singletonFamily\_fisher}]\label{thm:singleton}
The singleton family satisfies the Fisher hypotheses with $k = 1$, $\lambda = 0$, and attains
$m = n$: namely $n \le n$, $|S_i| = 1$ for all $i$, and $|S_i \cap S_j| = 0$ for all $i \ne j$, with
$0 = \lambda < k = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $S_i = \{i\}$ has cardinality $1$. For $i \ne j$, $\{i\} \cap \{j\} = \varnothing$ since a common
element would force $i = j$. The bound $n \le n$ is reflexive.
\end{proof}

Geometrically, the incidence vectors of the singletons are precisely the standard basis vectors
$e_1, \dots, e_n$ of $\R^n$, the maximal orthonormal system. The bound is tight, ruling out vacuity.

\subsection{Necessity: spectral degeneracy at $\lambda = k$}

\begin{proposition}[Necessity of $\lambda < k$, cf.\ \texttt{fisher\_lam\_lt\_k\_necessary} / \texttt{degenerate\_gram\_not\_posDef}]\label{prop:nec}
The hypothesis $\lambda < k$ cannot be relaxed to $\lambda \le k$. At $\lambda = k$ the Gram matrix
degenerates to $kJ$, which is not positive definite, and the size bound $m \le n$ fails.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\lambda = k$, two distinct $k$-element sets sharing $k$ points are equal, so the constraint is
degenerate. Algebraically, $(k-\lambda)I + \lambda J = kJ$. For any nonzero $x$ with
$\sum_i x_i = 0$ (existing when $m \ge 2$), $x^\top(kJ)x = k\big(\sum_i x_i\big)^2 = 0$, so $kJ$ has a
zero eigenvalue: positive semidefinite but not positive definite. The independence certificate is
lost. The strict inequality is exactly the threshold between a bounded and an unbounded family.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Hypothesis verification}
Given $n, k, \lambda$ and a family $F = [A_1, \dots, A_m]$, assert $0 \le \lambda < k$, check
$|A_i| = k$ for all $i$ and $|A_i \cap A_j| = \lambda$ for all $i < j$; if all hold, return the bound
$m \le n$. Complexity $O(m^2 n)$ with bitset intersections.

\subsection{Gram-spectrum certification}
Form $V$ (rows are incidence vectors), compute $G = VV^\top$, and certify positive definiteness via
$\min(\mathrm{eig}(G)) > 0$. For a valid uniform family the spectrum is $k - \lambda$ (multiplicity
$m-1$) and $k + (m-1)\lambda$ (multiplicity $1$). Complexity $O(m^2 n + m^3)$.

\section{Applications}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Combinatorial design theory:} caps the size of pairwise-balanced uniform families.
  \item \textbf{Coding theory:} bounds constant-weight codes with prescribed pairwise intersections.
  \item \textbf{Finite geometry:} restricts configurations of uniform incidence.
  \item \textbf{Design of experiments:} bounds the number of blocks in a balanced incomplete block
        design by the number of treatments.
  \item \textbf{Method transfer:} the encode $\to$ Gram $\to$ positive-definite $\to$ dimension
        pipeline is a reusable extremal proof strategy.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and Future Directions}

By decoupling the spectral engine (Theorems~\ref{thm:posdef}--\ref{thm:bound}) from the combinatorial
dictionary (Lemmas~\ref{lem:inner}--\ref{lem:innerself}), the same inequality discharges both an
indexed and an unindexed bound, and $\lambda < k$ is pinned down as the condition keeping the smallest
eigenvalue $k - \lambda$ strictly positive.

\paragraph{C1. Non-uniform Fisher via diagonal-dominant Gram positivity.}
If $A_1, \dots, A_m \subseteq [n]$ satisfy $|A_i \cap A_j| = \lambda \ge 1$ for $i \ne j$ with
arbitrary $|A_i| > \lambda$, then $m \le n$. The Gram matrix $D + \lambda J$ with
$D = \diag(|A_i| - \lambda) \succ 0$ is positive definite by the same additive split, so uniformity
is unnecessary; \texttt{Matrix.PosDef.diagonal} supplies the generalization.

\paragraph{C2. Modular Heged\H{u}s bound (Frankl--Wilson mod a prime).}
For a prime $p$, a family with $|A_i| \equiv 0$ and $|A_i \cap A_j| \not\equiv 0 \pmod p$ for
$i \ne j$ has size at most $\sum_{i<p} \binom{n}{i}$. The real Gram argument is replaced by the rank
of the inclusion matrix over $\F_p$: ``positive definite'' becomes ``nonsingular mod $p$,'' an
eigenvalue $\to$ determinant migration.

\paragraph{C3. Equiangular lines from the eigenvalue gap.}
A set of $m$ unit vectors in $\R^n$ with pairwise inner products in $\{+\alpha, -\alpha\}$
($0 < \alpha < 1$) satisfies $m \le \frac{n(1-\alpha^2)}{1 - m\alpha^2}$ whenever $m\alpha^2 < 1$.
The Gram matrix $I + \alpha S$ ($S$ a $\pm 1$ hollow symmetric matrix) is positive definite iff the
smallest eigenvalue of $S$ exceeds $-1/\alpha$, a Perron--Frobenius estimate.

\paragraph{C4. Quantitative refinements.}
Dimension-explicit and stability versions (near-extremal families are near-singleton) tracking the
gap $k - \lambda$ are a natural continuation.

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a modular, fully verified derivation of a uniform Fisher / Frankl--Wilson bound from a
single eigenvalue inequality. The incidence-vector dictionary $\inner{v_A}{v_B} = |A \cap B|$ converts
the combinatorial constraints into the constant-pattern Gram matrix $(k-\lambda)I + \lambda J$, whose
positive definiteness for $0 \le \lambda < k$---Heged\H{u}s' eigenvalue condition---forces linear
independence and hence $m \le n$. The singleton family attains the bound; the degeneracy at
$\lambda = k$ shows the hypothesis is exactly necessary.

